\title{Finite Join Laws and Constructive Lower Bounds\\for the $\mathbb Z_2$ Co-Index}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 15, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a finite multi-join calculus for octahedral spheres with antipodal $\Ztwo$-actions. The central fact is that the join of octahedral spheres of dimensions $m$ and $n$ is antipodally isomorphic to the octahedral sphere of dimension $m+n+1$. Thus the join of factors of dimensions $d_0,\ldots,d_{p-1}$ has co-index $\sum_i d_i+p-1$. This proves permutation invariance and recovers repeated suspension by the zero-sphere. For an arbitrary free $\Ztwo$-space $K$, an equivariant map from the octahedral $a$-sphere to $K$ can be joined with itself to produce a map from the octahedral sphere of dimension $p(a+1)-1$ to the $p$-fold self-join of $K$. The resulting co-index lower bound is sharp for octahedral spheres. We give inductive proofs, computational algorithms, examples, applications, and directions for extending the construction.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The topological join combines two spaces and introduces an interpolation parameter. For spheres it obeys the familiar dimension rule
\[
\dim(X*Y)=\dim X+\dim Y+1.
\]
In equivariant topology, the join also combines equivariant maps, making it a mechanism for amplifying certificates of symmetry.

We study free actions of $\Ztwo$. The standard models are octahedral spheres, the boundaries of cross-polytopes under the antipodal action. Their combinatorial structure makes the join transparent: antipodal coordinate pairs from the two factors are concatenated. Iteration gives a complete finite multi-join law without requiring a new obstruction argument for each number of factors.

For a nonempty family $O_{d_0},\ldots,O_{d_{p-1}}$, we prove
\[
O_{d_0}*\cdots*O_{d_{p-1}}\cong O_{\sum_i d_i+p-1}
\]
equivariantly. Hence its co-index is exactly $\sum_i d_i+p-1$. The expression is symmetric in the dimensions, proving permutation invariance. Zero-dimensional factors recover suspension.

For an arbitrary free $\Ztwo$-space $K$, a witness $O_a\to K$ can be joined $p$ times to construct
\[
O_{p(a+1)-1}\longrightarrow K^{*p}.
\]
This separates witness construction, which provides a lower bound, from obstruction theory or structural classification, which is needed for equality. On octahedral targets, the exact structural identification proves sharpness.

\section{Definitions and basic principles}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{$\Ztwo$-space} is a space $K$ with an involution $x\mapsto -x$, so that $-(-x)=x$. The action is \emph{free} if $-x\ne x$ for every $x\in K$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
A map $f:K\to L$ of $\Ztwo$-spaces is \emph{equivariant} if $f(-x)=-f(x)$. An equivariant isomorphism is a bijective equivariant map with equivariant inverse.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
For $n\ge0$, the \emph{octahedral $n$-sphere} $O_n$ is the boundary of the $(n+1)$-dimensional cross-polytope. Its vertices are
\[
\{+e_0,-e_0,\ldots,+e_n,-e_n\},
\]
and a face contains at most one vertex from each antipodal pair. Its realization is $S^n$ with the antipodal action.
\end{definition}

Thus $O_0$ is a pair of points, $O_1$ is the boundary of a square, and $O_2$ is the boundary of an octahedron.

\begin{definition}
The \emph{$\Ztwo$ co-index} of a free $\Ztwo$-space $K$ is
\[
\coind(K)=\max\{n\in\mathbb N:O_n\to K\text{ equivariantly}\}.
\]
More generally, $\coind(K)\ge n$ denotes the existence of such a map whenever a finite maximum is not assumed.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Equivariant invariance]
If $K$ and $L$ are equivariantly isomorphic, then $\coind(K)=\coind(L)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Compose every map $O_n\to K$ with the isomorphism to transfer witnesses to $L$. Composition with the inverse transfers them back, so the same integers are admissible.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Normalization]
For every $n\ge0$, $\coind(O_n)=n$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The identity gives the lower bound. The standard antipodal obstruction excludes an equivariant map from a strictly higher-dimensional antipodal sphere to $S^n$, giving the upper bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{join} $K*L$ is the quotient of $K\times L\times[0,1]$ that collapses the $L$-coordinate at $t=0$ and the $K$-coordinate at $t=1$. It carries the involution
\[
-[x,y,t]=[-x,-y,t].
\]
For equivariant maps $f:K\to K'$ and $g:L\to L'$, the join map is
\[
(f*g)([x,y,t])=[f(x),g(y),t].
\]
\end{definition}

The join map is equivariant by direct substitution. Define $K^{*1}=K$ and $K^{*(p+1)}=K*K^{*p}$. Other parenthesizations are canonically equivariantly isomorphic.

\section{The octahedral join calculus}

\begin{theorem}[Binary Octahedral Join Theorem]
For all $m,n\ge0$, there is an equivariant isomorphism
\[
O_m*O_n\cong O_{m+n+1}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The first factor has $m+1$ antipodal vertex pairs and the second has $n+1$. In the simplicial join their vertex sets are disjoint, and a face is a union of one face from each factor. Hence a face selects at most one vertex from each of the combined $m+n+2$ pairs. This is precisely the face rule for $O_{m+n+1}$. Mapping the first and second blocks to consecutive coordinates gives a face-preserving bijection commuting with antipodes; the inverse block decomposition has the same properties.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Sharp Binary Join Law]
For all $m,n\ge0$,
\[
\coind(O_m*O_n)=m+n+1.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Use equivariant invariance and normalization on $O_{m+n+1}$.
\end{proof}

Say that $K$ has \emph{octahedral type $n$} when $K\cong O_n$ equivariantly. The preceding results provide a compact calculus: $O_n$ has type $n$; type $n$ determines co-index $n$; and the join of types $m$ and $n$ has type $m+n+1$. The final rule follows by joining the two isomorphisms and applying the binary theorem.

\section{Finite multi-joins}

For $p\ge1$ and nonnegative $d_0,\ldots,d_{p-1}$, let
\[
J(d_0,\ldots,d_{p-1})=O_{d_0}*(O_{d_1}*(\cdots*O_{d_{p-1}})).
\]

\begin{theorem}[Finite Octahedral Multi-Join Theorem]
There is an equivariant isomorphism
\[
J(d_0,\ldots,d_{p-1})\cong O_D,
\qquad D=\sum_{i=0}^{p-1}d_i+p-1.
\]
Consequently,
\[
\coind(J(d_0,\ldots,d_{p-1}))=\sum_{i=0}^{p-1}d_i+p-1.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $p$. The case $p=1$ is immediate. By induction, the final $p-1$ factors have octahedral type
\[
D'=\sum_{i=1}^{p-1}d_i+p-2.
\]
Joining the first factor and applying the binary theorem gives type
\[
d_0+D'+1=\sum_{i=0}^{p-1}d_i+p-1.
\]
Equivariant invariance and normalization give the co-index formula.
\end{proof}

Equivalently, factor $O_{d_i}$ contributes $d_i+1$ antipodal pairs. Their total is $\sum_i(d_i+1)$, and the corresponding octahedral dimension is one less.

\begin{corollary}[Permutation Invariance]
For every permutation $\sigma$ of $\{0,\ldots,p-1\}$,
\[
\coind(J(d_0,\ldots,d_{p-1}))
=
\coind(J(d_{\sigma(0)},\ldots,d_{\sigma(p-1)})).
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Both sides are the sum of the same dimensions plus $p-1$.
\end{proof}

In fact, every ordering is equivariantly isomorphic to the same $O_D$.

\begin{example}
For dimensions $(2,0,3,1)$, the co-index is
\[
2+0+3+1+4-1=9.
\]
Every ordering has this value.
\end{example}

\section{Suspension towers}

The suspension of $K$ is $K*S^0$, and $S^0=O_0$.

\begin{theorem}[Octahedral Suspension Tower]
For $n,k\ge0$, joining $O_n$ with $k$ copies of $O_0$ gives an octahedral sphere of dimension $n+k$. Hence
\[
\coind(O_n*\underbrace{O_0*\cdots*O_0}_{k\text{ copies}})=n+k.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply the multi-join theorem to dimensions $n,0,\ldots,0$. Their sum is $n$ and there are $k$ joins.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Repeated Zero-Sphere Join]
For $p\ge1$,
\[
\underbrace{O_0*\cdots*O_0}_{p\text{ factors}}\cong O_{p-1},
\qquad
\coind(O_0^{*p})=p-1.
\]
\end{corollary}

\section{Constructive bounds for arbitrary self-joins}

Let $K$ be a free $\Ztwo$-space and suppose an equivariant witness $f:O_a\to K$ is given.

\begin{lemma}[Join of Witnesses]
If $f:O_a\to K$ and $g:O_b\to L$ are equivariant, then an equivariant map
\[
O_{a+b+1}\longrightarrow K*L
\]
exists.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Join the maps to obtain $f*g:O_a*O_b\to K*L$, then precompose with the inverse of $O_a*O_b\cong O_{a+b+1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Constructive Self-Join Lower Bound]
If $p\ge1$ and an equivariant map $O_a\to K$ exists, then there is an equivariant map
\[
O_{p(a+1)-1}\longrightarrow K^{*p}.
\]
Consequently,
\[
\coind(K^{*p})\ge p(a+1)-1.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $p$. For $p=1$, the given map has the required source dimension. If a witness from $O_{p(a+1)-1}$ into $K^{*p}$ has been constructed, join it with the original map. The source dimension becomes
\[
a+\bigl(p(a+1)-1\bigr)+1=(p+1)(a+1)-1.
\]
This produces the next witness.
\end{proof}

The shifted form
\[
\coind(K^{*p})+1\ge p(a+1)
\]
shows that the certified shifted co-index grows linearly with the number of factors. The proof explicitly constructs the witness in $p-1$ join steps.

\section{Sharpness on the octahedral tower}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp Octahedral Self-Join Law]
For all $a\ge0$ and $p\ge1$,
\[
O_a^{*p}\cong O_{p(a+1)-1}
\]
equivariantly, and
\[
\coind(O_a^{*p})=p(a+1)-1.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply the multi-join theorem to $p$ factors of dimension $a$. Their dimension sum is $pa$ and there are $p-1$ joins, so $pa+p-1=p(a+1)-1$. Normalization gives equality of co-indices.
\end{proof}

For $a=0$ this is the classical $O_0^{*p}\cong O_{p-1}$. For $a=2$ and $p=4$, the exact co-index is $4(2+1)-1=11$.

For arbitrary $K$, the same argument gives only a lower bound: the target may receive a map from a still larger octahedral sphere. Octahedral targets are sharp because their complete type gives the matching upper bound.

\section{Algorithms and numerical realization}

\subsection{Finite multi-join evaluation}
Given a nonempty array $(d_0,\ldots,d_{p-1})$, accumulate its sum $S$ and length $p$, then return $S+p-1$. Correctness is Theorem 4.1. The procedure uses $O(p)$ time and $O(1)$ auxiliary space.

\subsection{Self-join certificate evaluation}
Given a certified dimension $a$ and positive count $p$, return $p(a+1)-1$. This takes $O(1)$ unit-cost arithmetic operations. Constructing the associated map requires $p-1$ join steps, with representation-dependent data complexity.

\subsection{Permutation audit}
For each candidate ordering, calculate its sum and length. Equal statistics imply equal co-index. Auditing $q$ arrays of length $p$ takes $O(qp)$ time. For actual permutations equality is automatic.

The first values of the sharp self-join formula are
\[
\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
 a\backslash p&1&2&3&4&5\\ \hline
0&0&1&2&3&4\\
1&1&3&5&7&9\\
2&2&5&8&11&14\\
3&3&7&11&15&19
\end{array}
\]
Rows are arithmetic progressions with common difference $a+1$; increasing $a$ by one raises a fixed column by $p$.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

\paragraph{Modular construction.}
If maps $O_{a_i}\to K_i$ are known for $0\le i<p$, joining them produces
\[
O_{\sum_i a_i+p-1}\longrightarrow K_0*\cdots*K_{p-1}.
\]
Thus independently certified components yield a global lower bound. Self-joins are the equal-factor specialization.

\paragraph{Suspension bookkeeping.}
Every suspension by $O_0$ raises octahedral co-index exactly once. This gives direct bookkeeping for towers formed by successively adding antipodal cone points.

\paragraph{Combinatorial models.}
Cross-polytope boundaries are finite complexes. Their joins are implemented by disjoint unions of signed vertex blocks, and counting antipodal pairs gives the dimension formula directly.

\paragraph{Grouping independence.}
Partition the $p$ factors into $s$ blocks. If block $j$ contains $q_j$ factors, its internal joins contribute $q_j-1$ dimensions, while joining the blocks contributes $s-1$. Since
\[
\sum_j(q_j-1)+(s-1)=p-1,
\]
the final dimension is independent of grouping. This explains parenthesization invariance numerically.

\paragraph{Construction versus obstruction.}
Functoriality of joins constructs lower-bound witnesses. Exact octahedral identification and the antipodal obstruction behind $\coind(O_n)=n$ provide upper bounds. Keeping these roles separate prevents an unsupported equality claim for a general target.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The shifted dimension $d+1$ counts antipodal coordinate pairs. Joining concatenates these pairs, so
\[
D+1=\sum_{i=0}^{p-1}(d_i+1),
\]
which is equivalent to $D=\sum_i d_i+p-1$. This one identity unifies binary joins, suspension towers, and repeated self-joins.

The exact formula relies on closure of octahedral spheres under joins. A witness $O_a\to K$ for a general $K$ need not determine its full co-index, so the self-join theorem gives only a lower bound. Determining when
\[
\coind(K^{*p})\ge p(\coind(K)+1)-1
\]
is sharp, and quantifying possible excess, are natural next problems. A heterogeneous theory should seek conditions for equality in
\[
\coind(K_0*\cdots*K_{p-1})\ge\sum_i a_i+p-1.
\]
Other directions include infinite joins with suitable topology, actions of finite groups beyond $\Ztwo$, explicit algorithms for constructing simplicial maps, and applications in which joins encode combined combinatorial constraints.

\section{Conclusion}

The binary structural identity
\[
O_m*O_n\cong O_{m+n+1}
\]
generates a complete finite calculus. It gives
\[
\coind(O_{d_0}*\cdots*O_{d_{p-1}})=\sum_i d_i+p-1,
\]
with permutation invariance and suspension as immediate consequences. For any free $\Ztwo$-space, a witness $O_a\to K$ powers up constructively to
\[
O_{p(a+1)-1}\to K^{*p}.
\]
On octahedral spheres the bound is exact. The theory reduces an entire family of iterated equivariant constructions to one structural isomorphism, one functorial operation, and transparent arithmetic.

\end{document}

